\section{Stability Regimes}

TSSE stability regimes fall into three primary classes:
\[
\mathcal{R}_{\text{TSSE}} =
\left\{
\mathcal{R}_{\text{T}},\;
\mathcal{R}_{\text{R}},\;
\mathcal{R}_{\text{F}}
\right\},
\]
where:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $\mathcal{R}_{\text{T}}$ is the temporal symmetry stability regime.
  \item $\mathcal{R}_{\text{R}}$ is the relativistic propulsion stability regime.
  \item $\mathcal{R}_{\text{F}}$ is the fusion energetic stability regime.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Temporal Stability Regime}

Temporal stability requires:
\[
\mathcal{T}\mathcal{U}(t) = \mathcal{U}(t)\mathcal{T}.
\]

\begin{lemma}[Temporal Stability]
If $[\mathcal{T},H_{\text{TSSE–AITFE}}] = 0$, then the system is temporally stable.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Commutation ensures temporal symmetry preservation under evolution.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Relativistic Stability Regime}

Relativistic stability requires:
\[
\mathcal{U}(t)^\top J\,\mathcal{U}(t) = J,
\qquad
\det(D\mathcal{U}(t)) = 1.
\]

\begin{lemma}[Relativistic Stability]
If $\mathcal{U}(t)$ is symplectic, then the system is relativistically stable.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Symplectic evolution preserves phase-space volume and canonical structure.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Fusion Stability Regime}

Fusion stability requires:
\[
G(q,p) = Q.
\]

\begin{lemma}[Fusion Stability]
If $H_{\text{FK}}$ is TSSE-admissible, then the fusion gain remains constant.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Fusion gain is defined as a ratio of TSSE-admissible Hamiltonians, which is
preserved under symplectic evolution.
\end{proof}

These lemmas establish the subsystem stability regimes of TSSE engines.

