\section{Perturbation Thresholds for TSSE Stability}

Let $\epsilon$ denote the perturbation magnitude introduced by stress Hamiltonians:
\[
H_{\epsilon} = H_{\text{TSSE–AITFE}} + \epsilon\,\Delta H.
\]

Define the stability threshold:
\[
\epsilon_{\text{max}} =
\sup\left\{
\epsilon \;\middle|\;
\delta_{\mathcal{T}}(t) = \mathcal{O}(\epsilon),\;
\delta_{\mathcal{S}}(t) = \mathcal{O}(\epsilon)
\right\}.
\]

\begin{definition}[TSSE Stability Threshold]
The TSSE stability threshold $\epsilon_{\text{max}}$ is the largest perturbation
magnitude for which temporal symmetry and manifold integrity remain within
TSSE-admissible bounds.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Perturbation Stability]
If $\epsilon < \epsilon_{\text{max}}$, then:
\[
\delta_{\mathcal{T}}(t),\;
\delta_{\mathcal{S}}(t)
=
\mathcal{O}(\epsilon).
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Follows from the linear scaling of invariant deviation under TSSE-admissible
perturbations.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[TSSE Stability Regime]
The TSSE system is stable if and only if:
\[
\epsilon < \epsilon_{\text{max}}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Stability requires bounded invariant deviation. This occurs precisely when
perturbations remain below the threshold.
\end{proof}

This theorem establishes the perturbation limits for TSSE stability.

